WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.000
The rules also changed what
the word boundary means.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.996
They're published as one
machine-readable ruleset,

3
00:00:05.996 --> 00:00:09.562
so the vocabulary can be counted
rather than characterized.

4
00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:18.599
In version 2026.10.13.01, the phrase
authorization boundary occurs zero times.

5
00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:23.695
The word boundary occurs eight times:
three times about the agency's own system,

6
00:00:23.695 --> 00:00:28.162
four times as a tag on rule families,
and once in supply-chain guidance.

7
00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:31.625
Those four families say what
a provider's boundary now is.

8
00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:34.550
Minimum Assessment Scope,
what is in the offering.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.600
Cryptographic Module Use.

10
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.225
Vulnerability Detection and Response.

11
00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:42.812
And Certification Data Sharing,
how the evidence reaches you.

12
00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:46.299
Four sets of obligations,
not a line on a diagram.

13
00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:50.075
The only boundary the rules name
is the agency's own system.

14
00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:54.025
Minimum Assessment Scope is a
test about data, not topology.

15
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:57.312
A resource is in the offering
if it's likely to handle

16
00:00:57.312 --> 00:01:01.288
federal customer data or affect
its security, wherever it sits.

17
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:06.348
And resources may vary by security category,
so a refusal that rests on "it would

18
00:01:06.348 --> 00:01:11.563
raise the whole environment's level" now
has to show which resource and which data.

19
00:01:11.563 --> 00:01:14.850
One line in that guidance
matters to every estate.

20
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.455
Software delivered for
installation on agency systems,

21
00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:22.827
and not operated in a shared-responsibility
model, typically agents,

22
00:01:22.827 --> 00:01:27.813
application clients and mobile apps, is
entirely outside the scope of FedRAMP.

23
00:01:27.813 --> 00:01:29.574
That doesn't make it unassessed.

24
00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:30.763
It makes it yours.

25
00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:35.533
So decide who owns each agent and
client, and cover it in the agency's

26
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:40.650
own authorization: its configuration,
its updates and the data it sends.

27
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.575
The authorizing official draws the boundary;
endpoint engineering does the work.

28
00:01:45.575 --> 00:01:48.328
Settle it at the next package update,

29
00:01:48.328 --> 00:01:52.610
and before the rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.
